  const rcPassages = [
    { id: "rc_1", title: "The Steam Engine and British Industry",
      text: "The standard narrative of the Industrial Revolution in Britain holds that the steam engine was the decisive technological driver of economic growth during the eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries. Recent scholarship, however, has complicated this picture. Economists who have attempted to measure productivity growth in British manufacturing during this period have found that, before 1830, the contribution of steam power to overall output was surprisingly modest. Water power, which was cheaper and already well established, continued to dominate many industries well into the 1820s. Even in cotton textiles—the sector most closely associated with the new technology—improvements in spinning and weaving machinery, rather than the engines that powered them, accounted for much of the measured productivity gains. The steam engine became indispensable only later, as factories moved away from rivers and as steamships and railways created demand for a powerful, portable source of energy. The case illustrates a general methodological caution: attributing economic outcomes to a single celebrated technology can obscure the complex interplay of energy costs, resource endowments, and incremental improvements in machinery that actually drives industrial change." },
    { id: "rc_2", title: "The Decline of the Honeybee",
      text: "Honeybee populations in North America and Europe have declined sharply since the 1980s, a phenomenon that has been attributed by scientists to a combination of factors rather than to any single cause. Parasitic mites, particularly Varroa destructor, weaken colonies and transmit viruses. Agricultural practices that reduce the diversity of forage—such as large-scale monoculture—leave bees with a narrow and nutritionally poor diet. Pesticides, including a class known as neonicotinoids, have been shown in laboratory studies to impair foraging behavior and navigation, although field evidence of their effect on whole colonies remains contested. Finally, changing climate patterns have shifted the timing of flowering plants, sometimes desynchronizing bloom from peak foraging periods. Because these pressures interact—a colony weakened by mites is more vulnerable to pesticide exposure, and a poorly nourished colony is less able to survive the winter—researchers argue that interventions aimed at a single factor are unlikely to reverse the decline. Conservation strategies that address habitat, pesticide use, and disease simultaneously, they conclude, offer the only realistic hope of stabilizing bee populations." },
    { id: "rc_3", title: "Literary Criticism and the Author",
      text: "In the mid-twentieth century, a school of literary criticism known as New Criticism insisted that the text itself should be the object of analysis, independent of the biographical circumstances of its author. Its practitioners championed close reading—the careful examination of tension, paradox, and irony within a work—and argued that appeals to an author's intentions amounted to a fallacy. For a time, biographical criticism fell into disrepute, dismissed by academic critics as unsophisticated. In recent decades, however, the pendulum has swung again. New historicist critics, while rejecting the notion that a text simply expresses an author's intentions, restored the importance of context, arguing that literature participates in—and is shaped by—the social and political currents of its time. Yet this renewed attention to context differs fundamentally from the older biographical method: where the earlier approach treated the author's life as the key that unlocked the meaning of a work, the newer one treats both author and work as embedded within a wider cultural field, so that the author becomes one interpretive context among many, rather than the source of final meaning." },
    { id: "rc_4", title: "Urban Green Space and Public Health",
      text: "Urban planners have long assumed that parks and green spaces improve the quality of city life, but only in recent years have researchers begun to quantify the health effects. Studies tracking large cohorts of city dwellers have found that residents living near substantial green areas report lower rates of cardiovascular disease and depression, even after controlling for income, education, and other socioeconomic variables. The mechanisms are not yet fully understood. Some evidence points to physical activity: people use parks for exercise, and proximity makes exercise more likely. Other findings emphasize psychological restoration, suggesting that even brief exposure to natural scenes reduces stress, as measured by cortisol levels and self-reported mood. A third hypothesis holds that green spaces encourage social interaction among neighbors, strengthening the social bonds associated with better health. Disentangling these pathways is difficult because they tend to occur together, and because neighborhoods with abundant green space are often simply more affluent. Nonetheless, the documented association has been consistent enough that several large cities have adopted policies to expand tree canopy in low-income districts, where green space is typically scarcest." },
    { id: "rc_5", title: "A Price History of Coffee",
      text: "Coffee is a textbook illustration of commodity price volatility. Because the trees take several years to mature and yields fluctuate with the weather, supply responds to price only after a delay, and this lag has repeatedly produced boom-and-bust cycles: high prices prompt new planting, and by the time the new trees bear fruit, supply has outstripped demand and prices collapse. Futures speculation amplifies the pattern, since traders betting on expected shortages bid prices upward before real scarcity arrives, encouraging still more planting.\n\nAttempts to stabilize the market have relied chiefly on export-limitation agreements among producing countries, under which signatories withhold a portion of their harvest to keep supply roughly in line with demand. Such pacts have proven fragile. Because they succeed only if every member complies, a single large producer can unravel one by selling beyond its quota, and consuming nations, which benefit from low prices, rarely enforce the limits. When an agreement has collapsed, the release of the withheld stock has often deepened the very slump it was meant to prevent.\n\nThe costs of this instability fall unevenly along the supply chain. Smallholder farmers, who usually lack storage and access to credit, must sell promptly at the prevailing price and thus absorb the full force of a downturn. Roasters in consuming countries, by contrast, can pass much of a price increase on to consumers, and they gain when the market falls, since their input costs drop well before retail prices do. The recurring cycles have therefore tended to shift margin away from growers toward the processors and distributors." },
    { id: "rc_6", title: "The Blind Cave Fish",
      text: "Several cave-dwelling populations of the freshwater fish Astyanax mexicanus have lost their functional eyes, the sockets reduced to vestiges buried beneath skin and scale. Genetic analysis shows that these cave forms descended from the sighted surface populations that still inhabit rivers and streams in the same region, and that eye loss has evolved independently in more than one cave lineage. Because eyelessness has arisen repeatedly, biologists regard the species as a natural experiment in repeated evolution.\n\nThe leading explanation is a trade-off in energy allocation. An eye costs energy to build and maintain, and where vision conveys no advantage, selection may favor fish that divert that energy to other uses; cave fish indeed show an enlarged olfactory system and expanded taste receptors. Embryonic studies support this view, showing eye loss to be an active developmental change in which the jaw and cranium are remodeled along with the shrinking eye. The two changes appear linked, genes affecting one also influencing the other, so that selection for a more efficient skull may carry eye loss along with it.\n\nNot all cave populations, however, have evolved in isolation. Periodic flooding carries surface fish into caves, and where cave and surface forms interbreed, genes for sightedness are reintroduced; the resulting hybrids possess eyes of intermediate size. That regressive traits nonetheless persist suggests that eyed fish do not simply dominate on contact, so the cave form's energy savings must offset any benefit restored vision confers. The persistence of eyelessness, in short, is itself evidence of the strength of the underlying trade-off." }
  ];

